  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 313 PM EDT Thu May 28 2026\n\nSaturday through Sunday Night...\n\nA very blocky upper level pattern will be in place across the region \nat the beginning of the period with closed lows located in the \nwestern CONUS and across southern New England. A stalled out \nboundary will remain in place from the northern Plains southeast \ninto the MO Valley and then down into the southeast.  A mid-level \nwave will move southeast across the southern US on Saturday which \nwill bring at least a chance of showers and a few storms to southern \nKentucky on Saturday.  Highs Saturday will remain in the mid-upper \n70s.  A drier period of weather is expected Saturday night with lows \nranging from the lower 50s in the Bluegrass to the upper 50s down \nacross southern KY.\n\nFor Sunday, stalled out boundary will remain in place across our far \nsouthwestern sections.  Another mid-level wave will rotate around \nthe southern ridge across the Gulf and will bring another chance of \nshowers a few storms to far southern KY.  Highs will be seasonal \nhere with readings in the upper 70s to near 80 and overnight lows in \nthe mid-upper 50s across the Bluegrass to the upper 50s/lower 60s \nacross southern KY.\n\nMonday through Thursday...\n\nBlocky upper level pattern will persist into next week with the \nsoutheastern New England trough deepening across the NW Atlantic and \nthe western CONUS upper low moving through Montana and into the \nnorthern High Plains.  Strong ridging will be found across the upper \nMidwest and a strong northerly flow will push much drier air into \nthe Ohio Valley and shove the stationary front further south and \nsouthwest of the region.  Upper level ridging will gradually build \ninto the region leading to a drier period of weather from late \nMonday through the remainder of the forecast period.\n\nTemperatures through the period will remain near to slightly below \nnormal with highs in the upper 70s to the lower 80s with overnight \nlows in the 50s/60s.",
